# Sysible Server — make Ctrl+F1 / Ctrl+F2 / Ctrl+F4 work at the raw Linux console.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: the Linux virtual console (a physical / VM login at the getty,
# not SSH) cannot deliver Ctrl+Fn to applications — the kernel keyboard driver has
# no modified-key (CSI-u / modifyOtherKeys) protocol, so Ctrl+F1 is indistinguish-
# able from F1 and tmux's `bind -n C-F1` never fires there. That is exactly why the
# split-terminal hotkeys "didn't work" on the console.
#
# THE FIX (robust): make Ctrl+F1/F2/F4 emit tmux's PREFIX (Ctrl+a = \001) followed
# by the split key. tmux reads those as literal bytes under ANY terminal, so the
# splits work on the bare console.
#
# WHY NOT the xterm CSI sequences (ESC[1;5P …): at the raw console TERM=linux, and
# tmux's `extended-keys` parsing is gated to xterm*/tmux*/screen* terminal-features —
# it is NOT active for `linux`, so tmux would receive ESC[1;5P as literal bytes and
# never decode it to C-F1. That was the long-standing "Ctrl+F1 doesn't work on the
# console" bug. The prefix+key form below sidesteps terminal-feature negotiation
# entirely and drives the same tmux splits directly.
#   Ctrl+F1 → C-a |  (split left/right)   Ctrl+F2 → C-a -  (split top/bottom)
#   Ctrl+F4 → C-a X  (kill pane, no-confirm; see `bind X kill-pane` in tmux.conf)
#
# loadkeys merges this on top of the active keymap — it touches ONLY these three
# combinations, nothing else. Function-key keycodes: F1=59 F2=60 F3=61 F4=62.

control keycode 59 = F200
control keycode 60 = F201
control keycode 62 = F202

string F200 = "\001|"
string F201 = "\001-"
string F202 = "\001X"
